> Having said that, again, ".a'u nai ro'o" or ".au nai ro'o" both fit
> pretty well.

Because of the modifiers {ro'i) (emotional), {ro'o} (physical), {ro'e}
(mental), {ro'a} (social), and {ro'u} (sexual), attitudinals don't
just cover emotions. For instance, {oiro'o} would make a good "ouch".

> > suspicion,
>
> That's just .ii

Since suspicion is a specifically social fear, I recommend using the
social modifier to create {.ii ro'a}.

